 : (a a_1 : List.{u_1} α) → List.Sublist.{u_1} a a_1 → Prop},\n  motive List.nil.{u_1} List.nil.{u_1} ⋯ →\n    (∀ {l₁ l₂ : List.{u_1} α} (a : α) (a_1 : List.Sublist.{u_1} l₁ l₂),\n        motive l₁ l₂ a_1 → motive l₁ (List.cons.{u_1} a l₂) ⋯) →\n      (∀ {l₁ l₂ : List.{u_1} α} (a : α) (a_1 : List.Sublist.{u_1} l₁ l₂),\n          motive l₁ l₂ a_1 → motive (List.cons.{u_1} a l₁) (List.cons.{u_1} a l₂) ⋯) →\n        ∀ {a a_1 : List.{u_1} α} (t : List.Sublist.{u_1} a a_1), motive a a_1 t","unsafe":false,"value":null,"source":{"code":"/--\nThe first list is a non-contiguous sub-list of the second list. Typically written with the `<+`\noperator.\n\nIn other words, `l₁ <+ l₂` means that `l₁` can be transformed into `l₂` by repeatedly inserting new\nelements.\n-/\ninductive Sublist {α} : List α → List α → Prop\n  /-- The base case: `[]` is a sublist of `[]` -/\n  | slnil : Sublist [] []\n  /-- If `l₁` is a subsequence of `l₂`, then it is also a subsequence of `a :: l₂`. -/\n  | cons a : Sublist l₁ l₂ → Sublist l₁ (a :: l₂)\n  /-- If `l₁` is a subsequence of `l₂`, then `a :: l₁` is a subsequence of `a :: l₂`. -/\n  | cons₂ a : Sublist l₁ l₂ → Sublist (a :: l₁) (a :: l₂)","start":1072,"end":1085},"axioms":[]}